## DESCRIPTION
## DBsubject(Statistics)
## DBchapter(Sampling distributions)
## DBsection(Sample mean)
## Institution(The College of Idaho)
## Author(R Cruz, L Danielson, J Guild)
## Level(1)
## MO(1)
## TitleText1('The Basic Practice of Statistics')
## AuthorText1('David Moore')
## EditionText1('5e')
## Section1('11')
## Problem1('')
## KEYWORDS('statistic', 'parameter', 'sampling distribution', 'sample')

DOCUMENT();    # This should be the first executable line in the problem.

loadMacros('PGstandard.pl', 'PGML.pl', 'parserPopUp.pl', 'PGcourse.pl');

#  Setup

$mu    = random(0.7,  0.9, 0.1);
$sigma = random(0.15, 0.2, 0.01);

$n = ($sigma / 0.01)**2;

@a      = ("larger", "smaller");
$choose = random(0, 1);

$dd = DropDown([ 'smaller', 'larger', 'equal', 'the same' ], $choose);

BEGIN_PGML
The level of nitrogen oxides (NOX) in the exhaust of a particular car model
varies with mean [$mu] grams per mile  and standard deviation [$sigma] grams
per mile . 

a)  What sample size is needed so that the standard deviation of the sampling
distribution is 0.01 grams per mile ?

    ANSWER: [_]{$n}

b)    If a [$a[$choose]] sample is considered, the standard deviation for [` \bar x`]  
would be [_]{$dd}

END_PGML

BEGIN_PGML_SOLUTION
a) Recall that the standard deviation of the sample distribution [`\sigma_s`] is related
    to the population standard deviation using [``` \sigma_s = \frac{\sigma}{\sqrt{n}}```]
    Solving this for [`n`] results in 
    [``` n  = \left(\frac{\sigma}{\sigma_s}\right)^2 
        = \left(\frac{[$sigma]}{0.01}\right)^2 = [$n]```]

b) Note that since the standard deviation of the sample distribution is inversely
    proportional to the square root of [`n,`] then a [$a[$choose]]
    sample size results in a [$a[1-$choose]] standard deviation.

END_PGML_SOLUTION

ENDDOCUMENT();       # This should be the last executable line in the problem.